  '\n      :root {\n        --paper: #fbf7ec;\n        --margin-red: #e08a8a;\n        --ink: #1e3a5f;\n        --ink-dark: #16243b;\n        --pencil: #3a3a3a;\n        --highlight-yellow: #fff2a8;\n        --highlight-green: #d7f2d1;\n        --sticky-pink: #ffd9e8;\n        --sticky-blue: #d6ecff;\n        --sticky-orange: #ffe3c2;\n        --sticky-purple: #e6d9f7;\n        --sticky-teal: #cdf2ee;\n        --accent-red: #c0392b;\n        --accent-green: #2f7a3c;\n        --accent-purple: #6c3fa8;\n        --accent-teal: #0f7a72;\n      }\n      * {\n        box-sizing: border-box;\n      }\n      body {\n        margin: 0;\n        padding: 40px 0 80px;\n        background: #e9e2d0;\n        font-family: "Kalam", cursive;\n        color: var(--ink-dark);\n        display: flex;\n        flex-direction: column;\n        align-items: center;\n      }\n      .page {\n        width: 900px;\n        max-width: 94vw;\n        background: var(--paper);\n        background-color: var(--paper);\n        position: relative;\n        border-radius: 4px;\n        box-shadow:\n          0 12px 35px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25),\n          0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);\n        padding: 50px 55px 55px 90px;\n        margin-bottom: 35px;\n        transform: rotate(-0.3deg);\n      }\n      .page:nth-of-type(2n) {\n        transform: rotate(0.25deg);\n      }\n      .page::before {\n        content: "";\n        position: absolute;\n        left: 60px;\n        top: 0;\n        bottom: 0;\n        width: 2px;\n        background: var(--margin-red);\n        opacity: 0.55;\n      }\n      .page::after {\n        content: "";\n        position: absolute;\n        left: 18px;\n        top: 30px;\n        bottom: 30px;\n        width: 1px;\n        background: repeating-linear-gradient(\n          to bottom,\n          transparent 0 10px,\n          #b7b0a0 10px 11px\n        );\n        opacity: 0.5;\n      }\n      .title-block {\n        text-align: center;\n        margin-bottom: 10px;\n      }\n      .title-block h1 {\n        font-family: "Shadows Into Light", cursive;\n        font-size: 44px;\n        margin: 0 0 4px 0;\n        transform: rotate(-1deg);\n      }\n      .title-block .sub {\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 24px;\n        color: var(--ink);\n      }\n      .washi {\n        display: inline-block;\n        background: var(--sticky-orange);\n        padding: 2px 14px;\n        transform: rotate(-2deg);\n        box-shadow: 1px 1px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 20px;\n        margin-top: 8px;\n      }\n      h2.topic {\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-weight: 700;\n        font-size: 34px;\n        display: inline-block;\n        padding-bottom: 2px;\n        margin: 34px 0 6px 0;\n        border-bottom: 3px solid;\n      }\n      .topic-tag {\n        display: inline-block;\n        background: var(--sticky-blue);\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 18px;\n        padding: 2px 10px;\n        border-radius: 3px;\n        transform: rotate(-1.5deg);\n        margin-left: 10px;\n        box-shadow: 1px 1px 3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);\n      }\n      h3.sub-heading {\n        font-size: 23px;\n        color: var(--ink);\n        margin: 22px 0 6px 0;\n        font-weight: 700;\n        text-decoration: underline wavy var(--margin-red);\n      }\n      p,\n      li {\n        font-size: 19px;\n        line-height: 1.65;\n        color: var(--pencil);\n      }\n      ul,\n      ol {\n        margin: 6px 0 14px 0;\n        padding-left: 26px;\n      }\n      li {\n        margin-bottom: 6px;\n      }\n      .hl {\n        background: var(--highlight-yellow);\n        padding: 0 3px;\n        border-radius: 2px;\n      }\n      .hl-green {\n        background: var(--highlight-green);\n        padding: 0 3px;\n        border-radius: 2px;\n      }\n      .hl-purple {\n        background: var(--sticky-purple);\n        padding: 0 3px;\n        border-radius: 2px;\n      }\n      .bold-ink {\n        color: var(--ink);\n        font-weight: 700;\n      }\n      .arrow-note {\n        color: var(--accent-red);\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 20px;\n        font-weight: 700;\n      }\n      .code-box {\n        background: #223046;\n        color: #dbe9ff;\n        font-family: "Courier New", monospace;\n        font-size: 15px;\n        padding: 14px 18px;\n        border-radius: 8px;\n        margin: 12px 0 18px 0;\n        box-shadow: 3px 3px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);\n        transform: rotate(0.4deg);\n        white-space: pre-wrap;\n        word-break: break-word;\n        overflow-wrap: anywhere;\n        line-height: 1.5;\n        max-width: 100%;\n      }\n      .code-title {\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 16px;\n        color: #9fd3ff;\n        margin-bottom: 4px;\n        display: block;\n      }\n      .sticky {\n        display: inline-block;\n        vertical-align: top;\n        width: 210px;\n        min-height: 140px;\n        padding: 14px 14px 10px 14px;\n        margin: 8px 14px 18px 0;\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 19px;\n        line-height: 1.35;\n        box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);\n        border-radius: 2px;\n      }\n      .sticky.pink {\n        background: var(--sticky-pink);\n        transform: rotate(-2deg);\n      }\n      .sticky.blue {\n        background: var(--sticky-blue);\n        transform: rotate(1.5deg);\n      }\n      .sticky.orange {\n        background: var(--sticky-orange);\n        transform: rotate(-1.2deg);\n      }\n      .sticky.green {\n        background: var(--highlight-green);\n        transform: rotate(2deg);\n      }\n      .sticky.purple {\n        background: var(--sticky-purple);\n        transform: rotate(-1.6deg);\n      }\n      .sticky.teal {\n        background: var(--sticky-teal);\n        transform: rotate(1.2deg);\n      }\n      .sticky b {\n        display: block;\n        font-size: 22px;\n        margin-bottom: 3px;\n        border-bottom: 1px dashed;\n      }\n      .sticky-row {\n        display: flex;\n        flex-wrap: wrap;\n      }\n      .callout {\n        border: 2.5px dashed;\n        border-radius: 12px;\n        padding: 14px 18px;\n        margin: 16px 0;\n        background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4);\n        position: relative;\n      }\n      .callout .tag {\n        position: absolute;\n        top: -16px;\n        left: 18px;\n        color: #fff;\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 18px;\n        padding: 2px 12px;\n        border-radius: 10px;\n        transform: rotate(-2deg);\n      }\n      .callout p {\n        margin: 6px 0;\n      }\n      .example-box {\n        border-left: 4px solid var(--accent-green);\n        background: #f2f9f0;\n        padding: 10px 16px;\n        margin: 10px 0 16px 0;\n        border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\n      }\n      .example-box .ex-label {\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-weight: 700;\n        color: var(--accent-green);\n        font-size: 20px;\n      }\n      .flow {\n        display: flex;\n        align-items: center;\n        flex-wrap: wrap;\n        gap: 6px;\n        margin: 14px 0;\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 20px;\n      }\n      .flow .step {\n        background: #fff;\n        border: 2px solid var(--ink);\n        border-radius: 10px;\n        padding: 8px 14px;\n        box-shadow: 2px 2px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);\n      }\n      .flow .arrow {\n        color: var(--accent-red);\n        font-size: 24px;\n      }\n      table.resp-table,\n      table.cmp-table {\n        width: 100%;\n        border-collapse: collapse;\n        margin: 10px 0 20px 0;\n        font-size: 17px;\n      }\n      table.resp-table th,\n      table.resp-table td,\n      table.cmp-table th,\n      table.cmp-table td {\n        border: 2px solid var(--ink);\n        padding: 8px 10px;\n        text-align: left;\n      }\n      table.resp-table th,\n      table.cmp-table th {\n        background: var(--sticky-blue);\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 20px;\n      }\n      table.resp-table td.char {\n        font-family: "Courier New", monospace;\n        font-weight: bold;\n        font-size: 20px;\n        text-align: center;\n        color: var(--accent-red);\n      }\n      .interview-star {\n        background: var(--sticky-pink);\n        border-radius: 10px;\n        padding: 14px 18px;\n        margin: 18px 0;\n        box-shadow: 2px 3px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);\n        transform: rotate(-0.4deg);\n      }\n      .interview-star .star-title {\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-weight: 700;\n        font-size: 22px;\n        color: var(--accent-red);\n      }\n      .interview-star p {\n        margin: 6px 0 0 0;\n      }\n      .page-footer {\n        text-align: center;\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 18px;\n        color: #8a8060;\n        margin-top: 30px;\n      }\n      .divider {\n        text-align: center;\n        color: #b7b0a0;\n        letter-spacing: 8px;\n        margin: 20px 0;\n        font-size: 18px;\n      }\n      .glossary-item {\n        margin-bottom: 16px;\n        padding-bottom: 12px;\n        border-bottom: 1px dashed #b7b0a0;\n      }\n      .glossary-item .term {\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-weight: 700;\n        font-size: 24px;\n        color: var(--accent-red);\n      }\n      .glossary-item .meaning {\n        font-size: 18px;\n        line-height: 1.55;\n      }\n      .struct-diagram {\n        display: flex;\n        flex-wrap: wrap;\n        gap: 0;\n        margin: 14px 0 20px 0;\n        font-family: "Courier New", monospace;\n        font-size: 14px;\n      }\n      .struct-diagram .field {\n        border: 2px solid var(--ink);\n        padding: 10px 8px;\n        text-align: center;\n        background: #fff;\n      }\n      .struct-diagram .field .bits {\n        font-family: "Caveat", cursive;\n        font-size: 15px;\n        color: var(--accent-purple);\n        display: block;\n        margin-top: 4px;\n      }\n      .phase-divider {\n        width: 900px;\n        max-width: 94vw;\n        text-align: center;\n        margin: 10px 0 25px 0;\n        font-family: "Shadows Into Light", cursive;\n        font-size: 30px;\n        color: #8a8060;\n      }\n\n      /* Color variants per phase */\n      .c-red {\n        color: var(--accent-red);\n        border-color: var(--accent-red);\n      }\n      .c-purple {\n        color: var(--accent-purple);\n        border-color: var(--accent-purple);\n      }\n      .c-teal {\n        color: var(--accent-teal);\n        border-color: var(--accent-teal);\n      }\n      .callout.c-red {\n        border-color: var(--accent-red);\n      }\n      .callout.c-red .tag {\n        background: var(--accent-red);\n      }\n      .callout.c-purple {\n        border-color: var(--accent-purple);\n      }\n      .callout.c-purple .tag {\n        background: var(--accent-purple);\n      }\n      .callout.c-teal {\n        border-color: var(--accent-teal);\n      }\n      .callout.c-teal .tag {\n        background: var(--accent-teal);\n      }\n      .sticky b.c-red {\n        color: var(--accent-red);\n        border-color: var(--accent-red);\n      }\n      .sticky b.c-purple {\n        color: var(--accent-purple);\n        border-color: var(--accent-purple);\n      }\n      .sticky b.c-teal {\n        color: var(--accent-teal);\n        border-color: var(--accent-teal);\n      }\n    ';
export const redisNotesMarkup =
  '\n    \u003c!-- ============ COVER ============ --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="title-block"\u003e\n        \u003ch1 class="c-red"\u003eRedis se Seekhna 🐧\u003c/h1\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sub"\u003e\n          Complete Interview-Ready Notes — Phase 1, 2 \u0026amp; 3\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="washi"\u003e\n          📌 TCP/RESP → Concurrency Models → Event Loops/epoll/kqueue → robj,\n          Memory, Persistence\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="divider"\u003e✦ Index ✦\u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eIntro\u003c/b\u003e — Redis Kya Hai? Kyun \u0026amp; Kahan Use Hota Hai + Key\n          Features\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003ePhase 1\u003c/b\u003e — Topic 1: TCP Echo Server | Topic 2: RESP Protocol\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003ePhase 2\u003c/b\u003e — Topic 3: Traditional DB vs Redis Concurrency | Topic\n          4: Event Loops \u0026amp; Single-Threaded Speed | Topic 5: epoll \u0026amp;\n          kqueue System Calls (Implementation + Cross-Platform)\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003ePhase 3\u003c/b\u003e — Topic 6: Redis Objects (robj) | Topic 7: Memory —\n          Allocators \u0026amp; Intset | Topic 8: Approximation Algorithms (LRU, LFU,\n          HyperLogLog, GeoHash) | Topic 9: Pipelining, Persistence \u0026amp;\n          Shutdown\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\u003cb\u003eLast Page\u003c/b\u003e — Glossary of heavy/technical words\u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— cover —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- ============ NEW INTRO PAGE: WHAT IS REDIS ============ --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-red"\u003e\n        Intro\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\u003eRedis Kya Hai? Aur Kyun Use Hota Hai?\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRedis\u003c/span\u003e ka full form hai\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eRE\u003c/span\u003emote \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eDI\u003c/span\u003ectionary\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eS\u003c/span\u003eerver. Yeh ek\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eopen-source, in-memory data store\u003c/span\u003e hai —\n        matlab yeh apna saara data (mostly) \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eRAM\u003c/span\u003e mein\n        rakhta hai, disk par nahi. Isi wajah se iski read/write speed\n        traditional databases (jo disk par data rakhte hain) se\n        \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003ebohot zyada fast\u003c/span\u003e hoti hai — microseconds\n        ka kaam.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Structure ke hisaab se Redis ko\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eKey-Value Store\u003c/span\u003e kaha jaata hai — har piece\n        of data ek unique \u003cb\u003ekey\u003c/b\u003e ke against store hota hai, jaise ek\n        dictionary (isiliye naam mein "Dictionary Server" bhi hai).\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eSimple analogy →\u003c/span\u003e Traditional database\n        (MySQL/Postgres) ek \u003cb\u003elibrary ki almari\u003c/b\u003e jaisa hai — organized,\n        detailed, par kitaab dhoondne mein thoda time lagta hai. Redis ek\n        \u003cb\u003echhota sa sticky-note board\u003c/b\u003e jaisa hai jo aapke haath ke bilkul\n        paas hai — "key bolo, value turant mil jaayegi", koi disk-seek ka\n        jhanjhat nahi.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e🎯 Redis Kis Liye Use Hota Hai?\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Redis ko usually ek \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eprimary/main database\u003c/span\u003e ki\n        tarah use nahi kiya jaata — balki ek\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003esupporting/helper layer\u003c/span\u003e ki tarah, jo\n        application ko fast aur scalable banane mein madad karta hai:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky orange"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e🚀 Caching\u003c/b\u003e\n          Sabse common use-case. Database se baar-baar same data padhne ke\n          badle, ek baar padh ke Redis mein "cache" kar do — agli baar seedha\n          RAM se milega, DB par load kam.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky blue"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e🔑 Session Store\u003c/b\u003e\n          User login sessions ko Redis mein rakhna — fast lookup, aur multiple\n          servers ke beech session share karna aasan ho jaata hai.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky green"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e📨 Message Broker / Pub-Sub\u003c/b\u003e\n          Redis ka Pub/Sub feature real-time messaging ke liye use hota hai —\n          jaise chat apps, live notifications.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky pink"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e🏆 Leaderboards / Counters\u003c/b\u003e\n          Sorted Sets se real-time leaderboards (games, ranking systems) bohot\n          efficiently banaye ja sakte hain.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky purple"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e⏳ Rate Limiting\u003c/b\u003e\n          "Ek user 1 minute mein sirf 10 requests bhej sakta hai" jaisa logic\n          Redis ke counters aur expiry se easily implement hota hai.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky teal"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e📋 Queues\u003c/b\u003e\n          Lists ka use karke simple background job queues banaye jaate hain\n          (jaise "process this task later").\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e✨ Key Features — Ek Nazar Mein\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eIn-Memory Speed:\u003c/span\u003e Sara data RAM mein,\n          isliye read/write operations \u003cspan class="hl"\u003emicroseconds\u003c/span\u003e mein\n          complete hote hain.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRich Data Structures:\u003c/span\u003e Sirf plain\n          key-value nahi — Strings, Lists, Sets, Sorted Sets, Hashes, Bitmaps,\n          HyperLogLogs, aur Geospatial indexes bhi support karta hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eSingle-Threaded, Phir Bhi Super Fast:\u003c/span\u003e\n          Ek hi thread par chalta hai (koi lock/context-switch overhead nahi),\n          par event-loop + epoll/kqueue jaisi techniques se hazaaron concurrent\n          clients handle kar leta hai\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e(poora detail Phase 2 mein aayega 👀)\u003c/span\u003e.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ePersistence Options:\u003c/span\u003e Memory volatile\n          hoti hai, isliye Redis RDB (snapshot) aur AOF (append-only log) se\n          data ko disk par bhi durable bana sakta hai\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e(Phase 3, Topic 9 mein detail)\u003c/span\u003e.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ePub/Sub Messaging:\u003c/span\u003e Publishers aur\n          Subscribers ke beech real-time messages bhejne ka built-in system.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eAtomic Operations:\u003c/span\u003e Ek time par ek hi\n          command execute hoti hai, isliye operations naturally atomic hote hain\n          — race conditions ki chinta nahi.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eTTL / Expiry Support:\u003c/span\u003e Har key par expiry\n          time set kiya ja sakta hai — time khatam hote hi key automatically\n          delete ho jaati hai (caching/session ke liye perfect).\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eReplication \u0026amp; Clustering:\u003c/span\u003e\n          High-availability aur horizontal scaling ke liye Redis master-replica\n          setup aur clustering support karta hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eLightweight Protocol (RESP):\u003c/span\u003e Apna khud\n          ka simple, fast wire-protocol use karta hai — bina JSON/XML ke parsing\n          overhead ke \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e(Phase 1, Topic 2 mein detail)\u003c/span\u003e.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-red"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e💡 Yaad Rakhne Wali Baat\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Redis \u003cb\u003edatabase ka replacement nahi\u003c/b\u003e hai — yeh usually\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003edatabase ke saath milkar\u003c/span\u003e kaam karta hai, taaki\n          repeated/hot data fast mile aur main database par load kam pade.\n          Real-world apps mein aap "Redis + Postgres/MySQL" jaisa combo bohot\n          common dekhoge.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e⭐ Interview Point\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eQ:\u003c/b\u003e Redis ko "Data Structure Server" bhi kaha jaata hai, sirf\n          "Key-Value Store" kyun nahi?\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eA:\u003c/b\u003e Kyunki value sirf ek plain string nahi ho sakti — value khud\n          ek List, Set, Hash, Sorted Set, ya Bitmap jaisi rich structure ho\n          sakti hai, jispar Redis apne special commands (jaise\n          \u003ccode\u003eLPUSH\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eSADD\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eZADD\u003c/code\u003e) se seedha\n          operate kar sakta hai — isiliye "Data Structure Server" naam zyada\n          accurate hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— intro —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003cdiv class="phase-divider"\u003e\n      — PHASE 1 : Networking \u0026amp; Communication Foundation —\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 1 --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-red"\u003e\n        Topic 1 \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\u003eTCP Echo Server\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Redis banane ka \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eStep 0\u003c/span\u003e hota hai — ek basic\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003enetworking foundation\u003c/span\u003e taiyar karna. Hum Node.js\n        ke \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003enative module\u003c/span\u003e se (bina external\n        dependency) ek \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eTCP Echo Server\u003c/span\u003e banate\n        hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e Client bhejta hai "hello",\n        server wahi "hello" wapas bhej deta hai. Isi ko \u003ci\u003eecho\u003c/i\u003e kehte hain.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e1️⃣ Server kaise start hota hai\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eFlags:\u003c/span\u003e Port (default\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e7379\u003c/span\u003e) aur Host (default\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e0.0.0.0\u003c/span\u003e — internet se kahin bhi accept karne\n          ke liye) command-line flags se set hote hain.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eListening:\u003c/span\u003e\n          \u003ccode\u003enet.createServer().listen(port, host)\u003c/code\u003e call hote hi OS\n          socket open kar deta hai — server ab us port par sun raha hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e2️⃣ Double For-Loop Architecture\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003eOuter Loop — naye clients accept karna\u003c/span\n        \u003ewhile (true) { const conn = listener.acceptSync(); // 🛑 Blocking Call!\n        // ... connection handle karo }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="arrow-note"\u003e➜\u003c/span\u003e \u003ccode\u003elistener.acceptSync()\u003c/code\u003e ek\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eblocking call\u003c/span\u003e hai — jab tak client na aaye, CPU\n        yahin ruka rehta hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003eInner Loop — ek client se lagatar padhna\u003c/span\n        \u003ewhile (true) { const msg = readCommandSync(conn); // 🛑 Blocking Read!\n        respond(conn, msg); }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Jaise hi client connect hota hai, hum isi inner loop mein us client ke\n        commands padhte-padhte respond karte rehte hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-red"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🚨 Bottleneck\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eClient A\u003c/b\u003e connect hota hai → server outer loop se inner loop\n          mein chala jaata hai → ab \u003ccode\u003ereadCommand\u003c/code\u003e par block ho gaya\n          (Client A ke data ka wait).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Isi waqt agar \u003cb\u003eClient B\u003c/b\u003e connect karna chahe —\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003enahi kar payega!\u003c/span\u003e Kyunki single thread toh\n          Client A ke andar phasa hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Client B, OS ke\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003enetwork backlog queue\u003c/span\u003e mein wait karega —\n          jab tak Client A disconnect na ho.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e⭐ Interview Point\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eQ:\u003c/b\u003e Agar single thread blocked hai, toh kya naye connections\n          accept ho sakte hain?\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eA:\u003c/b\u003e Synchronous single-threaded architecture mein\n          \u003cu\u003ebilkul nahi\u003c/u\u003e — single thread ek hi blocking call (read/accept)\n          mein phas jaata hai. Isi bottleneck ko hatane ke liye hum aage\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eAsynchronous I/O + epoll\u003c/span\u003e use karenge\n          (Topic 4-5 👀).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 1 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 2 --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-red"\u003e\n        Topic 2 \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\u003eRESP Protocol\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Jab CLI mein \u003ccode\u003eSET k v\u003c/code\u003e likhte ho, wo text seedha server ko\n        nahi jaata — pehle ek special format mein\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eserialize\u003c/span\u003e hota hai jise\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRESP (Redis Serialization Protocol)\u003c/span\u003e kehte\n        hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003eJSON / XML kyun nahi?\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky orange"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e❌ JSON\u003c/b\u003e\n          Parsing CPU-heavy — har quote ", colon : aur escape character ko scan\n          karna padta hai.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky green"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003e✅ RESP\u003c/b\u003e\n          Ultra-simple, human-readable, almost-zero networking overhead —\n          isiliye highly performant.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003eGeneral Rule\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        RESP ek \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRequest–Response\u003c/span\u003e protocol hai.\n        Golden rule:\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\n          \u003ehar data type ek special character se shuru hota hai\u003c/span\n        \u003e, aur har block \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e\\r\\n\u003c/span\u003e (carriage return + line\n        feed) par khatam hota hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ctable class="resp-table"\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eType\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eChar\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eFormat\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eExample\u003c/th\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eSimple String\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd class="char"\u003e+\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e+text\\r\\n\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003e+OK\\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003e+PONG\\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eError\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd class="char"\u003e-\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e-msg\\r\\n\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003e-ERR Key not found\\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eInteger\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd class="char"\u003e:\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e:num\\r\\n\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003e:1729\\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eBulk String\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd class="char"\u003e$\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e$len\\r\\ndata\\r\\n\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003e$4\\r\\npong\\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eArray\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd class="char"\u003e*\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e*count\\r\\n...elements\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eneeche dekho 👇\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n      \u003c/table\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e🌟 Bulk String ($) — sabse important\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003eFormat: \u003ccode\u003e$ [length in bytes] \\r\\n [actual string] \\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e string "pong" ke liye bhejenge:\n        \u003ccode\u003e$4\\r\\npong\\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-red"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e💡 Binary Safety\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Simple String (+) \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ebinary-safe nahi\u003c/span\u003e hoti — agar\n          string ke andar khud \\r\\n aa jaye, parser samjhega string wahin khatam\n          ho gayi → data truncate!\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Bulk String isko solve karti hai: length pehle hi bata di jaati hai\n          ($4), toh parser exact utne bytes utha leta hai — chahe andar null\n          character ho ya raw image (PNG/JPEG) bytes.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky blue"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003eEmpty String\u003c/b\u003e\n          $0\\r\\n\\r\\n\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky pink"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003eNull String\u003c/b\u003e\n          $-1\\r\\n (yahan -1 = null)\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003eArrays (*)\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Format: \u003ccode\u003e* [number of elements] \\r\\n\u003c/code\u003e phir back-to-back saare\n        elements.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e Array bhejna hai:\n        \u003ccode\u003e["a", 200, "cat"]\u003c/code\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e*3\\r\\n $1\\r\\na\\r\\n :200\\r\\n $3\\r\\ncat\\r\\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky orange"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003eNull Array\u003c/b\u003e\n          *-1\\r\\n\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky green"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003eEmpty Array\u003c/b\u003e\n          *0\\r\\n\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="divider"\u003e✦ ✦ ✦\u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        🧠 Low-Level Parser: Prefix Length Optimization\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Jaise hi parser \u003ccode\u003e$\u003c/code\u003e ke baad \u003ccode\u003e5\u003c/code\u003e padhta hai →\n          usko turant pata chal jaata hai aage 5 bytes ki string aa rahi hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Memory mein \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eexact 5 bytes ka buffer\u003c/span\u003e allocate\n          hota hai → koi dynamic reallocation kharcha nahi.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Parser ko stream ka har byte check nahi karna padta — seedha\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003etargeted read\u003c/span\u003e laga kar data utha leta\n          hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003eJavaScript Decode Logic\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003ccode\u003edecodeOne(data []byte)\u003c/code\u003e → stream mein se sirf\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003epehla valid RESP element\u003c/span\u003e decode karta hai,\n        aur return karta hai:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky blue"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003eValue\u003c/b\u003e\n          Actual decoded data\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky pink"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-red"\u003eDelta ✨\u003c/b\u003e\n          Kitne bytes process ho chuke — "magic variable"\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003epos = pos + delta\u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Isi delta ki madad se loop \u003ccode\u003edata[pos:]\u003c/code\u003e slice leke aage\n        badhta hai — \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003ebina memory copy kiye\u003c/span\u003e poora\n        stream sequentially parse ho jaata hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e✅ Phase 1 Complete!\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Revise karo: TCP blocking model (accept/read blocking, single-thread\n          bottleneck) + RESP ke 5 types (+ - : $ *) + Bulk String binary safety\n          + Delta-based parsing.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="arrow-note"\u003e➜ Next:\u003c/span\u003e Topic 3 — Traditional DB vs\n          Redis Concurrency, phir Event Loops \u0026amp; epoll/kqueue internals 🚀\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 2 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003cdiv class="phase-divider"\u003e\n      — PHASE 2 : The Heart of Redis — Concurrency Models \u0026amp; Event Loops —\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 3 (was Topic 5) --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-purple"\u003e\n        Topic 3\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\n          \u003eTraditional Databases vs Redis — Concurrency Models\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Aage badhne se pehle samajh lete hain ki\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003etraditional databases\u003c/span\u003e (jaise MySQL ya\n        Postgres) concurrency ko kaise handle karti hain — aur Redis ka poora\n        design isi approach ke \u003cspan class="hl"\u003enuksaan se seekh kar\u003c/span\u003e bana\n        hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e1️⃣ The Rule: "Naya Client? Naya Thread!"\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Traditional databases ka simple rule hota hai — jaise hi ek naya client\n        connect hota hai, database uske liye ek\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ealag OS thread\u003c/span\u003e spawn kar deta hai. Agar\n        Client A aur Client B dono connect karte hain, toh Thread A aur Thread B\n        banenge, aur yeh dono\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ealag-alag CPU cores par parallel\u003c/span\u003e chal sakte\n        hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e 100 clients connected hain toh\n        database ke andar (roughly) 100 threads chal rahe hain, sab apna-apna\n        kaam parallel kar rahe hain.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Sunne mein yeh perfect lagta hai (real parallelism!), lekin iske\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003edo bade structural nuksan\u003c/span\u003e hain:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky pink"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-purple"\u003e🔒 Locking Nightmare\u003c/b\u003e\n          Shared data par race condition. Fix karne ke liye mutex/semaphore\n          lagane padte hain → jo lock leta hai wahi chalta hai, baaki queue mein\n          rukte hain.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky orange"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-purple"\u003e🔁 Context-Switch Cost\u003c/b\u003e\n          CPU ko hazaron threads ke beech register state save/load karni padti\n          hai — yeh "juggling" hi CPU cycles khaa jaati hai.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🧮 Locking Nightmare — Full Example\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Maan lijiye ek shared global variable hai \u003ccode\u003ecount = 10\u003c/code\u003e.\n          Client A ka thread aur Client B ka thread dono ek sath\n          \u003ccode\u003ecount++\u003c/code\u003e chalana chahte hain.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003ccode\u003ecount++\u003c/code\u003e asal mein\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eek atomic step nahi\u003c/span\u003e hota — internally 3 steps\n          hain: (1) memory se count padho → 10, (2) usko +1 karo → 11, (3) wapas\n          memory mein likho.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Agar dono threads ek sath step (1) kar lein — dono ne \u003cb\u003e10\u003c/b\u003e padha\n          — toh dono apna answer \u003cb\u003e11\u003c/b\u003e hi likhenge. Final value \u003cb\u003e11\u003c/b\u003e ho\n          jayegi, jabki sahi answer \u003cb\u003e12\u003c/b\u003e hona chahiye tha (ek increment\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003egum ho gaya\u003c/span\u003e — "lost update" bug).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Isko rokne ke liye developer ko\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003emutex lock\u003c/span\u003e lagana padta hai: jab tak\n          Thread A apna read-modify-write poora na kar le, Thread B ko wait\n          karna padta hai.\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\n            \u003eMatlab locks lagte hi asli parallelism khatam ho jaata hai\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          — kaam phir se sequential ho jaata hai, bas overhead extra add ho\n          gaya!\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        2️⃣ Synchronous Blocking — Single-Thread Ka Sabse Bada Khatra\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Ab lagta hai "chalo threads ka jhanjhat hi hata dete hain, sirf ek\n        single thread rakhte hain" — lekin agar normal (synchronous) network\n        programming mein sirf ek thread use karoge, toh server\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ebilkul fass jayega\u003c/span\u003e — jaisa Topic 1 mein humne\n        dekha tha.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        3️⃣ Redis Ka Solution — Event Loop + Async I/O (Preview)\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Redis inn dono problems (locking nightmare \u003ci\u003eaur\u003c/i\u003e blocking) ko ek hi\n        chaal se solve karta hai:\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eAsynchronous I/O + I/O Multiplexing\u003c/span\u003e (isko\n        "Apparent Concurrency" bhi kehte hain — lagta hai parallel ho raha hai,\n        par asal mein single thread hi hai — isi ko aage\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eTopic 4-5\u003c/span\u003e mein detail se samjhenge).\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        4️⃣ Memory vs Network — The Ultimate Cheat Code 💡\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Redis ek aur hardware-level truth ka fayda uthata hai:\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\n          \u003eNetwork I/O bohot slow hota hai, lekin RAM par chalne wale\n          (in-memory) operations super-fast hote hain\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n        — microseconds/nanoseconds ka kaam.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🎯 Isse do behtareen fayde milte hain\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003e1. No Locks Needed:\u003c/b\u003e Jab kaam nanoseconds mein ho raha ho aur ek\n          waqt mein sirf ek hi command chal rahi ho, toh mutex/lock ki zaroorat\n          hi khatam — code simple + lightning fast.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003e2. Guaranteed Atomicity:\u003c/b\u003e Kyunki ek time par ek hi command\n          execute hoti hai, har operation naturally atomic ho jaata hai — race\n          conditions ki chinta hi nahi karni padti (upar wala\n          \u003ccode\u003ecount++\u003c/code\u003e wala bug yahan possible hi nahi hai).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e⭐ Interview Point — Traditional vs Redis\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eQ:\u003c/b\u003e Redis single-threaded hoke bhi MySQL jaise multi-threaded DB\n          se fast kyun feel hota hai simple ops ke liye?\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eA:\u003c/b\u003e Kyunki Redis RAM mein kaam karta hai (nanoseconds), locks ki\n          zaroorat hi nahi padti, aur kernel-level event notification\n          (epoll/kqueue) se ek hi thread hazaron connections handle kar leta hai\n          bina kisi ko block kiye — jabki traditional DB threads spawn karke\n          locking + context-switching ka overhead uthata hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 3 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 4 (was Topic 3) --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-purple"\u003e\n        Topic 4\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\u003eEvent Loops \u0026amp; Single-Threaded Speed\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Ab dekhte hain Redis ka sabse bada magic:\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003emulti-threading ke bina bhi\u003c/span\u003e, ek single\n        thread par chalte hue, hazaron concurrent clients ko bina block hue\n        handle kar leta hai. Isse pehle samajhna zaroori hai ki jab network se\n        data aata hai, tab hardware aur OS ke beech asal mein hota kya hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🍽️ Sabse Pehle — Restaurant Waiter Analogy\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Socho ek restaurant hai jahan \u003cb\u003esirf ek hi waiter\u003c/b\u003e hai, lekin\n          \u003cb\u003e100 tables\u003c/b\u003e hain.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ePurana (blocking) tareeka:\u003c/span\u003e Waiter table\n          1 pe jaata hai, order leta hai, kitchen mein jaake khud khada rehta\n          hai jab tak khana ban na jaaye, phir serve karta hai — tab jaake table\n          2 pe jaata hai. Agar khana banne mein 10 minute lagte hain, waiter 10\n          minute \u003cspan class="hl"\u003esirf khada rehta hai, kuch nahi karta\u003c/span\u003e.\n          100 tables ke liye ye impossible hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRedis/epoll ka tareeka:\u003c/span\u003e Waiter table 1\n          pe order leta hai, kitchen ko de deta hai, aur\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eturant table 2 pe chala jaata hai\u003c/span\u003e. Wo sirf tab\n          kisi table pe wapas jaata hai jab kitchen se ghanti bajti hai "table 5\n          ka khana ready hai!". Ek hi waiter (single thread) 100 tables\n          (concurrent clients) ko handle kar leta hai kyunki wo kabhi bekar\n          khada nahi rehta.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003eepoll = wo ghanti system.\u003c/span\u003e\n          Kitchen (kernel) khud bata deta hai konsa order (socket) ready hai,\n          waiter ko har table pe jaake check nahi karna padta.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e1️⃣ Ek Network Packet Ka Poora Safar\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Data seedha aapke program mein nahi ghus jaata — uska ek pura pipeline\n        hota hai:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="flow"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e🔌 Network Card\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="arrow"\u003e→\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e⚡ Hardware Interrupt\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="arrow"\u003e→\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e🗄️ Kernel Buffer\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="arrow"\u003e→\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e📋 User Space Copy\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="arrow"\u003e→\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e🖥️ Aapka Code\u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eNetwork Card (Hardware):\u003c/span\u003e Packet sabse\n          pehle Wi-Fi/Ethernet card par hit karta hai — yeh physical layer hai,\n          purely electronics/signals ka kaam.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eHardware Interrupt:\u003c/span\u003e Card data receive\n          karte hi CPU ko ek \u003ci\u003einterrupt signal\u003c/i\u003e bhejta hai. CPU apna chal\n          raha kaam turant rok ke kernel ko control de deta hai ("Interrupt\n          Service Routine" chalti hai) taaki data turant uthaya ja sake — warna\n          packet loss ho sakta hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eKernel Buffer:\u003c/span\u003e Kernel data ko ek\n          \u003ci\u003esocket receive buffer\u003c/i\u003e (kernel space mein) mein daal deta hai.\n          Yeh area normal user-level program (jaise tumhara JS/Python code)\n          directly touch nahi kar sakta — security aur isolation ke liye.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eCPU Scheduling + User Space Copy:\u003c/span\u003e Jab OS\n          scheduler tumhare process ko CPU time deta hai, tabhi ek\n          \u003ccode\u003ecopy\u003c/code\u003e operation hoti hai — data kernel buffer se tumhare\n          program ke \u003ci\u003euser space socket buffer\u003c/i\u003e mein aata hai.\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\n            \u003eSirf tabhi tumhara \u003ccode\u003eread()\u003c/code\u003e call use dekh paata\n            hai.\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e Maan lo client "SET name John"\n        bhejta hai. (1) Ye data pehle Ethernet card pe electrical signal ke roop\n        mein aata hai. (2) Card CPU ko interrupt karta hai — "oye, data aaya\n        hai!" (3) Kernel ye data apne internal buffer mein rakh deta hai (aapka\n        JS code abhi isse touch nahi kar sakta). (4) Jab tak aap\n        \u003ccode\u003eread()\u003c/code\u003e call nahi karte, ye data waha hi pada rehta hai.\n        Yahan tak koi bhi "loop" nahi chal raha — ye sirf hardware+OS ka kaam\n        hai.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🤔 Yeh do copies kyun?\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Kernel space aur user space alag rakhne se ek buggy/malicious user\n          program directly hardware ya doosre process ka memory corrupt nahi kar\n          sakta. Yeh trade-off hai:\n          \u003cspan class="hl-purple"\u003esafety/isolation vs raw speed\u003c/span\u003e — isi\n          wajah se advanced systems (jaise io_uring) is copy-overhead ko kam\n          karne ki koshish karte hain, but woh ek aur level ki baat hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e2️⃣ The Heart of Asynchronous I/O 🧠\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Kyunki kernel khud data receive karta hai, use\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003epehle se pata hota hai\u003c/span\u003e ki kaunsa socket (File\n        Descriptor) ke liye data ready ho chuka hai.\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\n          \u003eYahi cheez epoll/kqueue ka dil aur dimaag hai.\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n        Kernel system calls ke zariye program ko bata sakta hai: "Bhai, yeh FD\n        ready hai, isko read kar lo."\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e📒 Kernel Ka Internal "Logbook"\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Socho kernel ke paas ek \u003cb\u003eregister/logbook\u003c/b\u003e hai jisme har socket\n          ki entry hai — kaunse FD mein data hai, kaunse mein nahi:\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eSocket (FD)\u003c/th\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eBuffer mein data hai?\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eFD 3 (Client A)\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eNahi\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eFD 4 (Client B)\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e\u003cb\u003eHaan, 200 bytes\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eFD 5 (Client C)\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eNahi\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eFD 6 (Client D)\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e\u003cb\u003eHaan, 50 bytes\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003c/table\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Jaise hi Client B ka packet network card se aata hai, kernel turant is\n          logbook mein FD 4 ke against "haan, data hai" likh deta hai. Ye update\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ehardware interrupt ke through hota hai\u003c/span\u003e,\n          real-time mein — kisi ko poochna nahi padta.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        Do Tareeke — Program Ko Kaise Pata Chale Ki Kaun Ready Hai?\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky orange"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-purple"\u003e❌ Tareeka 1: Polling\u003c/b\u003e\n          Program khud har FD ke paas jaake poochta rahe: "FD 3, data hai? Nahi?\n          FD 4, hai? Nahi?..." — 10,000 sockets ho aur sirf 2 ready ho, tab bhi\n          10,000 baar poochna padta hai. Yehi C10K problem ki jad hai.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky green"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-purple"\u003e✅ Tareeka 2: epoll\u003c/b\u003e\n          Program kernel ko ek baar bolta hai "in FDs ko watch karo", phir sirf\n          ek call karta hai — kernel apne aap sirf ready FDs ki chhoti list\n          turant de deta hai. Koi wasted checking nahi.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003ePolling — Bura Tareeka (illustrative)\u003c/span\n        \u003ewhile (true) { for (let fd of allSockets) { // maano 10,000 sockets\n        hain if (hasData(fd)) { // ye ek system call hai — EXPENSIVE read(fd); }\n        } }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003eepoll — Achha Tareeka\u003c/span\u003econst readyFds =\n        epoll_wait(epollFd, events, -1); // readyFds = [FD 4, FD 6] ← sirf yahi\n        2 jo actually ready hain!\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eZaroori clarity →\u003c/span\u003e Event loop koi\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ealag thread ya process nahi\u003c/span\u003e hai! Yeh tumhare hi\n        main thread ke andar ek patli layer hai jo lagatar kernel se poochti\n        rehti hai:\n        \u003ci\n          \u003e"Bhaiya, koi data ready hai kya? Ready hai toh do, warna main ruka\n          hoon (block)."\u003c/i\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🔄 Thread Ki Do States — Block vs Active\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003eEvent loop ka poora code sirf ye hai:\u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n          while (true) { ready_list = epoll_wait(); // \u0026lt;-- yahan thread RUK\n          jaata hai (block) har ready FD ke liye kaam karo; // \u0026lt;-- yahan\n          thread ACTIVE hai }\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003e1. Blocked state\u003c/b\u003e — \u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait()\u003c/code\u003e ke andar. Thread\n          literally so raha hai, OS ne usse CPU se hata diya hai,\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e0% CPU use ho raha hai\u003c/span\u003e. Jab tak koi socket\n          ready na ho, kernel isse jagayega hi nahi.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003e2. Active state\u003c/b\u003e — jab \u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/code\u003e kuch return karta\n          hai, thread turant jaagta hai, ready FDs ko process karta hai\n          (read/write/business logic), aur kaam khatam hote hi\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ewapas\u003c/span\u003e \u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/code\u003e pe chala jaata\n          hai, phir se so jaata hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eReal timeline example:\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eTime\u003c/th\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eKya hota hai\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e10:00:00.000\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eepoll_wait() call, thread block ho jaata hai\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e10:00:03.500\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eClient A ka data aaya, kernel ne thread ko jagaya\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e10:00:03.501\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003ethread ne Client A ka data process kiya (0.5ms laga)\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e10:00:03.502\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003ethread wapas epoll_wait() pe gaya, phir block\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e10:00:07.900\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eClient B aur D dono ka data ek saath aaya\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e10:00:07.903\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003edono process karke wapas epoll_wait() pe, phir block\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003c/table\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Dekho — 10:00:03.502 se 10:00:07.900 tak (~4.4 second) thread\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ekuch nahi kar raha tha\u003c/span\u003e, lekin CPU pe wo koi\n          resource waste nahi kar raha — wo poori tarah so raha hai, jaise\n          waiter kitchen ki ghanti ka wait kar raha ho, table pe khada nahi hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003eSynchronous vs Event-Loop — farak kya hai?\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eCheez\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eTopic 1 (Blocking, per-client loop)\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eTopic 4 (Event Loop + epoll)\u003c/th\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eKitne threads\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e1 thread, per-connection block ho jaata hai\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e1 thread hi, par kabhi ek client ke liye ruka nahi rehta\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eNaya client aaye toh?\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003ePurana client free hone tak wait karta hai\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\n            epoll turant dono ko track karta hai, jo ready ho wahi serve hota\n            hai\n          \u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eCPU waste\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eBlocking call mein CPU literally ruka rehta hai\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eepoll_wait sirf tab wake hota hai jab kaam ho\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eScale\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e~100s clients tak thik, fir bottleneck (C10K problem)\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eHazaaron clients ek thread mein aaram se\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n      \u003c/table\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e💾 Concrete Number Se Feel Karo\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Har OS thread ka stack size default ~1-8 MB hota hai. 10,000 threads =\n          \u003cb\u003e10,000 × 2MB = 20 GB RAM\u003c/b\u003e sirf thread stacks ke liye! Jabki\n          epoll model mein 1 thread + ek chhota si struct per connection (~kuch\n          KB) — total memory footprint sirf chand sau MB.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e⭐ C10K Problem (context)\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Purane servers jab ek thread-per-connection model use karte the,\n          10,000 concurrent connections handle karna mushkil ho jaata tha\n          (thread creation + context-switching costly hai). epoll/kqueue jaisi\n          event-driven techniques isi \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eC10K problem\u003c/span\u003e ko\n          solve karne ke liye bani thi — Redis, Nginx, Node.js sab isi\n          philosophy par chalte hain.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 4 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 5 (merged: was Topic 4 + Topic 6) --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-purple"\u003e\n        Topic 5\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\n          \u003eepoll \u0026amp; kqueue — Implementation + Cross-Platform\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Ab dekhte hain yeh event loop\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ecode mein kaise implement\u003c/span\u003e hota hai. Unix\n        philosophy — \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e"everything is a file"\u003c/span\u003e — har socket\n        connection ek integer se represent hota hai jise\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eFile Descriptor (FD)\u003c/span\u003e kehte hain. Linux par\n        hum teen main system calls se apna Custom Event Loop banate hain:\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eepoll_create1 → epoll_ctl → epoll_wait\u003c/span\u003e.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="flow"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e1️⃣ epoll_create1\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="arrow"\u003e→\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e2️⃣ epoll_ctl (register)\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="arrow"\u003e→\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="step"\u003e3️⃣ epoll_wait (loop forever)\u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eOverview →\u003c/span\u003e Yeh teen steps kyun hain?\n        Kyunki epoll use karne ka process teen alag kaamon mein bata hai:\n        \u003cb\u003eepoll_create1\u003c/b\u003e = ek "control room" banao, \u003cb\u003eepoll_ctl\u003c/b\u003e = us\n        control room mein bataओ kis-kis ko watch karna hai, aur\n        \u003cb\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/b\u003e = control room mein baith ke wait karo ki kab koi\n        ready ho.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e1️⃣ epoll_create1 — The Initialization\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003eSabse pehle kernel ke andar ek epoll \u003ci\u003einstance\u003c/i\u003e banate hain:\u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003eJavaScript\u003c/span\u003econst epollFd =\n        epoll.create1(0);\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eKya ho raha hai:\u003c/span\u003e Ye call kernel se bolti\n        hai "mere liye ek naya \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eepoll instance\u003c/span\u003e bana do."\n        Kernel apne andar ek data structure banata hai jisme wo saari sockets ki\n        list track karega jo aap monitor karna chahte ho.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eReturn value:\u003c/span\u003e Ek naya file descriptor\n        (\u003ccode\u003eepollFd\u003c/code\u003e) — ye khud epoll instance ko uniquely identify\n        karta hai. Dhyan do, ye kisi client ka FD nahi hai, ye us\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003epoore watchlist system\u003c/span\u003e ka FD hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eAnalogy →\u003c/span\u003e Socho aap ek restaurant khol\n        rahe ho aur aapne ek \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003econtrol room\u003c/span\u003e banaya\n        jahan saari tables ki ghantiyon ke signals aakar dikhenge.\n        \u003ccode\u003eepoll_create1\u003c/code\u003e sirf usi control room ko banane jaisa hai —\n        abhi tak koi table connect nahi hui hai, room bas khali khada hai.\n        \u003cdiv class="code-box" style="margin-top: 8px"\u003e\n          epoll_create1() → [ khaali watchlist room, ID = epollFd ]\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        Ye sirf \u003cb\u003eek baar\u003c/b\u003e call hota hai, server start hote waqt — jab tak\n        server chal raha hai, yehi ek epoll instance use hota rehta hai.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e2️⃣ epoll_ctl — The Registration\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Ab kernel ko batana padta hai ki kaun-kaunse sockets monitor karne hain:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\n          \u003eJavaScript — listening socket register karna\u003c/span\n        \u003econst event = { events: epoll.EPOLLIN, // "read-ready" event ke liye\n        interested hoon fd: listenFd, }; epoll.ctl(epollFd, epoll.EPOLL_CTL_ADD,\n        listenFd, event);\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eKya ho raha hai:\u003c/span\u003e Ab hum us control room\n        (\u003ccode\u003eepollFd\u003c/code\u003e) ko batate hain: "is \u003ccode\u003elistenFd\u003c/code\u003e socket\n        ko watchlist mein daal do, aur mujhe tab batao jab isme read karne layak\n        (\u003ccode\u003eEPOLLIN\u003c/code\u003e) data aaye." Server ka\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eMain Listening Socket\u003c/span\u003e (port 7379) yahaan\n        register hota hai aur \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eEPOLLIN\u003c/span\u003e (read event) par\n        set hota hai. Matlab:\n        \u003ci\u003e"Jab bhi koi naya client connect hona chahe, mujhe notify karna."\u003c/i\u003e\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky blue"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-purple"\u003eEPOLL_CTL_ADD\u003c/b\u003eNaya FD watchlist mein daalo —\n          jaise naya client connect hua\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky orange"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-purple"\u003eEPOLL_CTL_MOD\u003c/b\u003eExisting FD ke events change karo\n          — ab write bhi watch karna hai\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky pink"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-purple"\u003eEPOLL_CTL_DEL\u003c/b\u003eFD ko watchlist se hatao — client\n          disconnect ho gaya\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample flow →\u003c/span\u003e Server start hote waqt,\n        sirf \u003cb\u003elistening socket\u003c/b\u003e register hota hai:\n        \u003ccode\u003eepoll_ctl(ADD, listenFd, EPOLLIN)\u003c/code\u003e — matlab "control room,\n        jab bhi koi naya client connect karne ki koshish kare, mujhe batana." Ab\n        jab ek \u003cb\u003enaya client actually connect ho jaata hai\u003c/b\u003e (\u003ccode\n          \u003elistenFd\u003c/code\n        \u003e\n        pe event fire hua), tab hum (1) \u003ccode\u003eaccept()\u003c/code\u003e call karke us naye\n        client ka apna FD nikalte hain, aur (2) us naye FD ko bhi turant\n        \u003ccode\u003eepoll_ctl(ADD, newClientFd, EPOLLIN)\u003c/code\u003e se watchlist mein daal\n        dete hain. Ab epoll dono ko — listening socket \u003ci\u003eaur\u003c/i\u003e saare client\n        sockets ko — ek saath track karta hai.\n        \u003cdiv class="code-box" style="margin-top: 8px"\u003e\n          Time 0: watchlist = [listenFd] Client A connects → watchlist =\n          [listenFd, fdA] Client B connects → watchlist = [listenFd, fdA, fdB]\n          Client A disconnects → watchlist = [listenFd, fdB]\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        Is tarah watchlist \u003cspan class="hl"\u003edynamically\u003c/span\u003e badhti-ghatti\n        rehti hai jaise clients aate-jaate hain — jaise har naye customer ki\n        table pe ek ghanti connect karte jaana, aur jaate waqt uski ghanti\n        control room se disconnect (DEL) kar dena.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e3️⃣ epoll_wait — The Traffic Controller 🚦\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Yeh event loop ka\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003esabse zaroori blocking call\u003c/span\u003e hai:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003eJavaScript\u003c/span\u003econst events = new Array(100);\n        const n = epoll.wait(epollFd, events, -1); // -1 = hamesha ke liye wait\n        karo for (let i = 0; i \u003c n; i++) { const fd = events[i].fd; // is fd par\n        kaam karo (accept ya read) }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eKya ho raha hai:\u003c/span\u003e Ye actual\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eblocking call\u003c/span\u003e hai — jahan thread "so jaata hai"\n        jab tak watchlist mein se koi bhi socket ready nahi ho jaata.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eParameters samjho:\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003ccode\u003eepollFd\u003c/code\u003e — kis control room ko check karna hai;\n        \u003ccode\u003eevents\u003c/code\u003e — ek khaali array jisme kernel ready events bharega;\n        \u003ccode\u003e-1\u003c/code\u003e (timeout) — matlab "hamesha ke liye wait karo, koi time\n        limit nahi." (Agar yahan \u003ccode\u003e1000\u003c/code\u003e daalte to 1 second baad bhi\n        return ho jaata chahe kuch ready na ho.)\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eReturn value:\u003c/span\u003e \u003ccode\u003en\u003c/code\u003e = kitne FDs\n        ready hain. Sirf \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ewahi FDs\u003c/span\u003e jo actually ready hain\n        — poori list nahi, koi polling nahi.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eConcrete example →\u003c/span\u003e Maano watchlist mein\n        10,000 clients hain, lekin abhi sirf 2 ne data bheja hai:\n        \u003cdiv class="code-box" style="margin-top: 8px"\u003e\n          n = epoll.wait(epollFd, events, -1); // n = 2 // events[0].fd = fdA\n          (Client A ready hai) // events[1].fd = fdB (Client B ready hai)\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        Baaki 9,998 clients ke baare mein kuch bhi check nahi hua — kernel ne\n        sirf apni pehle se maintained list se seedha ye 2 nikaal ke de diye.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eAnalogy →\u003c/span\u003e Control room mein baitha\n        operator hamesha ghanti bajne ka wait kar raha hai\n        (\u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/code\u003e). Jaise hi ek ya do ghantiyaan bajti hain, wo\n        unhi tables ka order leke jaata hai, kaam khatam karke wapas control\n        room mein aake phir se wait karta hai.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Yeh call \u003cspan class="hl"\u003etab tak block rehti hai\u003c/span\u003e jab tak\n          registered sockets (10 ho ya 10,000) mein se \u003cb\u003ekoi ek\u003c/b\u003e bhi ready\n          nahi ho jaata.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Jaise hi kisi client ne data bheja, kernel use apne buffer mein le\n          leta hai aur \u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/code\u003e turant unblock ho jaata hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Yeh sirf ek\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003echhoti si list (events buffer)\u003c/span\u003e return\n          karta hai — sirf wahi FDs jo sach me ready hain.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="hl-green"\n            \u003eFuzool ke inactive connections par loop chalakar CPU waste nahi\n            hota\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          — yehi is design ka sabse bada fayda hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e📎 -1 timeout ka matlab\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003ccode\u003eepoll.wait(epollFd, events, -1)\u003c/code\u003e mein \u003ccode\u003e-1\u003c/code\u003e ka\n          matlab hai "infinite wait" — jab tak koi event na aaye tab tak block\n          raho. Isko positive number (milliseconds) doge toh timeout ke baad bhi\n          return ho jaayega, chahe koi event na ho (useful jab background timers\n          bhi run karne ho).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-purple"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🔗 Teeno Steps Ek Saath\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n          epoll_create1() → Control room banao (ek baar, startup pe) ↓\n          epoll_ctl(ADD, listenFd) → Listening socket register karo (ek baar) ↓\n          while (true) { epoll_wait() ← yahan baar-baar ready FDs milte hain\n          naya client? → accept() + epoll_ctl(ADD, newFd) ← naya FD register\n          hota hai loop ke andar purana client ready? → read() + process +\n          respond }\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Isliye pehle 2 steps (\u003ccode\u003ecreate1\u003c/code\u003e, pehla \u003ccode\u003ectl\u003c/code\u003e)\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003esetup phase\u003c/span\u003e hain — ek baar hote hain.\n          Teesra step (\u003ccode\u003ewait\u003c/code\u003e) aur uske andar naye FDs ke liye\n          \u003ccode\u003ectl\u003c/code\u003e calls — ye \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eruntime loop\u003c/span\u003e\n          hai jo server ke poore life mein baar-baar chalta rehta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        Level-Triggered vs Edge-Triggered (bonus depth)\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eMode\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eBehaviour\u003c/th\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\u003cspan class="hl-purple"\u003eLevel-Triggered (default)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\n            Jab tak socket buffer mein data pada hai, epoll_wait\n            \u003cb\u003ebaar-baar\u003c/b\u003e notify karta rahega — simpler to reason about,\n            Redis jaise systems mostly isi ko prefer karte hain.\n          \u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eEdge-Triggered (EPOLLET)\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003e\n            Sirf \u003cb\u003estate change\u003c/b\u003e par ek baar notify karta hai (not-ready →\n            ready). Fast hai par tumhe\n            \u003ci\u003esab data ek hi baar mein poori tarah read karna padta hai\u003c/i\u003e,\n            warna miss ho sakta hai.\n          \u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n      \u003c/table\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e Maan lo client A ne 1000 bytes\n        bheje lekin aapne sirf 200 bytes read kiye ek call mein. Level-Triggered\n        mode mein: agla \u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/code\u003e phir se bolega "fdA ready hai!"\n        kyunki abhi bhi 800 bytes buffer mein pade hain — simple, kam se kam\n        padho, epoll baar-baar yaad dilata rehta hai. Edge-Triggered mode mein:\n        sirf ek baar batayega jab data "not-ready → ready" hua — agar saara data\n        ek hi baar mein nahi padha to baaki 800 bytes ka notification\n        \u003cb\u003emiss\u003c/b\u003e ho jaayega jab tak naya data na aaye, isliye us mode mein\n        loop lagakar poora data khinchna padta hai (\u003ccode\n          \u003ewhile(read() \u003e 0)\u003c/code\n        \u003e).\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        4️⃣ JavaScript Custom Abstraction — FD Wrapper 🛠️\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Normal Node.js programming mein hum \u003ccode\u003enet.Socket\u003c/code\u003e use karte\n        hain — yeh raw file descriptors ko direct touch nahi karne deta. Lekin\n        deep internals ke liye humein raw system calls\n        (\u003ccode\u003efs.readSync\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003efs.writeSync\u003c/code\u003e — jo directly ek FD\n        par kaam karte hain) chalane padte hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Iske liye code clean rakhne ke liye ek custom class banate hain —\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eFDConnection\u003c/span\u003e (ya kisi bhi\n        Readable/Writable "stream-like" interface jaisa kuch):\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003eJavaScript — FD ko wrap karna\u003c/span\u003eclass\n        FDConnection { constructor(fd) { this.fd = fd; } read(buffer) { const n\n        = fs.readSync(this.fd, buffer); return n; } write(buffer) { const n =\n        fs.writeSync(this.fd, buffer); return n; } }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eKyun important hai →\u003c/span\u003e Tumhara\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003epura purana business logic\u003c/span\u003e (decode, parse,\n        response likhna) already ek common \u003ccode\u003eread()\u003c/code\u003e/\u003ccode\n          \u003ewrite()\u003c/code\n        \u003e\n        interface par likha hua tha. Ab bas ek naya class (jo raw FD ko wrap\n        karta hai) usi interface ko implement kar deta hai —\n        \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003ebusiness logic ek line bhi change nahi hoti\u003c/span\n        \u003e, aur hum aaram se synchronous socket se asynchronous FD-based socket\n        par switch ho jaate hain!\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="divider"\u003e✦ ✦ ✦\u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003ePoora Event Loop — ek nazar mein\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\u003ePseudo-flow (JavaScript)\u003c/span\u003econst epollFd =\n        epollCreate1(); epollCtl(ADD, listenFd, EPOLLIN); while (true) { const n\n        = epollWait(epollFd, events, -1); // block yahin hota hai — sabke liye\n        ek jagah for (const fd of events.slice(0, n)) { if (fd === listenFd) {\n        const newConn = accept(listenFd); epollCtl(ADD, newConn, EPOLLIN); //\n        naya client bhi watchlist mein } else { const data = read(fd); // sirf\n        ready client se read respond(fd, process(data)); } } }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Bas \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eek hi thread\u003c/span\u003e, ek hi loop — par kabhi bhi\n        kisi ek client ke wait mein poora system nahi rukta. Yehi Redis ki\n        single-threaded speed ka raaz hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        5️⃣ Linux Ke Baahar — kqueue \u0026amp; Windows IOCP\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003ccode\u003eepoll\u003c/code\u003e Linux-specific hai. Doosre platforms par\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ebilkul wahi kaam\u003c/span\u003e karne wale apne-apne\n        mechanisms hote hain — conceptual flow same rehta hai: kernel se\n        notification lena ki kaunsa FD I/O ke liye ready hai.\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\n          \u003eSirf syntax, function names, aur arguments ka fark hota hai.\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003ePlatform Mapping →\u003c/span\u003e Linux →\n        \u003ccode\u003eepoll\u003c/code\u003e | macOS/BSD → \u003ccode\u003ekqueue\u003c/code\u003e | Windows →\n        \u003ccode\u003eIOCP\u003c/code\u003e (I/O Completion Ports)\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003ePlatform\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eMechanism\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eCore Idea\u003c/th\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eLinux\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eepoll (epoll_create1 / epoll_ctl / epoll_wait)\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd rowspan="3"\u003e\n            Kernel registered FDs ko track karta hai aur sirf ready ones ki list\n            deta hai — koi polling/wasted loop nahi.\n          \u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003emacOS / BSD\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003ekqueue\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eWindows\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eIOCP\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n      \u003c/table\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e⭐ Phase 2 Interview-Ready Highlights\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          1. Event loop koi jadui thread nahi — single thread par chalne wala\n          loop hai jo \u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/code\u003e ke notifications par kaam karta\n          hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          2. \u003cb\u003ePacket Lifecycle:\u003c/b\u003e Hardware Card → Interrupt → Kernel Buffer\n          → User Space Copy.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          3. \u003cb\u003eCore System Calls:\u003c/b\u003e \u003ccode\u003eepoll_create1\u003c/code\u003e (init) →\n          \u003ccode\u003eepoll_ctl\u003c/code\u003e (register/deregister FDs) →\n          \u003ccode\u003eepoll_wait\u003c/code\u003e (block until ready, return only ready FDs).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          4. Business logic common \u003ccode\u003eread()\u003c/code\u003e/\u003ccode\u003ewrite()\u003c/code\u003e\n          abstraction ki wajah se blocking se async switch karne par change nahi\n          hoti.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          5. Level-triggered vs Edge-triggered ka trade-off samajhna —\n          simplicity vs raw performance.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          6. Yeh koi Redis-specific trick nahi —\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRedis, Node.js, aur Python AsyncIO\u003c/span\u003e sab\n          isi general OS-level pattern (epoll/kqueue/IOCP) ko reuse karke ek hi\n          thread par bina locks ke hazaron concurrent clients handle karte hain.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 5 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003cdiv class="phase-divider"\u003e\n      — PHASE 3 : Redis Internals \u0026amp; Persistence —\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 6 (was Topic 7) --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-teal"\u003e\n        Topic 6\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\n          \u003eRedis Objects (robj) — Har Value Ka Wrapper\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Jab aap Redis mein koi key-value pair store karte ho, Redis value ko\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eraw format mein nahi rakhta\u003c/span\u003e. Har value ek\n        ultra-optimized wrapper structure mein pack hoti hai jise\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRedis Object (robj)\u003c/span\u003e kehte hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        robj Ka 12-Byte Structure (RAM Bachane Ka Dimaag)\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        C language ke \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ebit fields\u003c/span\u003e ka smart use karke\n        Redis is poore object ko sirf \u003cb\u003e12 bytes\u003c/b\u003e mein squeeze kar deta hai:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="struct-diagram"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="field" style="width: 80px"\u003e\n          Type\u003cspan class="bits"\u003e4 bits\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="field" style="width: 100px"\u003e\n          Encoding\u003cspan class="bits"\u003e4 bits\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="field" style="width: 150px"\u003e\n          LRU/LFU\u003cspan class="bits"\u003e24 bits (3 bytes)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="field" style="width: 130px"\u003e\n          Refcount\u003cspan class="bits"\u003e4 bytes\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="field" style="width: 180px"\u003e\n          *Ptr (pointer)\u003cspan class="bits"\u003e4 ya 8 bytes\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eType (4 bits):\u003c/span\u003e Value kis type ki hai —\n          String, List, Set, Hash.\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eInteresting fact:\u003c/span\u003e Redis ke paas alag se\n          "Integer" type nahi hota — sab kuch String type ke andar hi handle\n          hota hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eEncoding (4 bits):\u003c/span\u003e Memory mein data ka\n          \u003ci\u003ephysical layout\u003c/i\u003e kya hai — normal raw bytes hain ya optimized\n          form (jaise integer array/intset).\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eLRU/LFU bits (24 bits):\u003c/span\u003e Last access time\n          ya frequency data — memory full hone par eviction (purani keys delete\n          karna) ke liye use hota hai (poora detail Topic 8 mein).\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eRefcount (4 bytes):\u003c/span\u003e Garbage collection\n          ke liye — kitne places se yeh object reference ho raha hai. Jab yeh\n          \u003cb\u003ezero\u003c/b\u003e ho jaata hai, object memory se free ho jaata hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ePtr (void pointer):\u003c/span\u003e Directly actual\n          value ko memory mein point karta hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-teal"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🔢 INCR Command Internally Kya Karta Hai\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Jab aap \u003ccode\u003eINCR key\u003c/code\u003e chalate ho, Redis pehle check karta hai\n          — kya key ka encoding pehle se \u003cb\u003einteger\u003c/b\u003e hai?\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Agar \u003cb\u003ehaan\u003c/b\u003e: string → integer convert → CPU level par\n          \u003ccode\u003ecount++\u003c/code\u003e → wapas string format mein convert → RAM mein\n          overwrite.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Agar key exist hi nahi karti: Redis default \u003ccode\u003e0\u003c/code\u003e value ka\n          naya object banata hai, use increment karke store kar deta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e \u003ccode\u003eSET counter 5\u003c/code\u003e phir\n        \u003ccode\u003eINCR counter\u003c/code\u003e chalane par: encoding check → integer hai → 5\n        → 6 → wapas robj mein store, sab kuch microseconds mein.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 6 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 7 (was Topic 8) --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-teal"\u003e\n        Topic 7\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\n          \u003eMemory Management — Allocators \u0026amp; Intset\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Kyunki Redis \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003epoora RAM par\u003c/span\u003e chalta hai,\n        iska hardware-level memory management bohot tight hona zaroori hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        1️⃣ zmalloc — Fragmentation Se Bachne Ka Custom Wrapper\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Operating system memory ko bade blocks (pages, standard \u003cb\u003e4KB\u003c/b\u003e) mein\n        allocate karta hai. Jab hum bohot saare chhote variables baar-baar\n        create/delete karte hain, toh beech-beech mein khaali jagah (vacuum) ban\n        jaati hai — isko\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eMemory Fragmentation\u003c/span\u003e kehte hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-teal"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e⚠️ Fragmentation Ka Real Problem\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Fragmentation ki wajah se\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eactual data kam hone par bhi\u003c/span\u003e, OS ko lagta hai\n          ki RAM poori bhar chuki hai — kyunki bache hue chhote-chhote free\n          chunks ek badi allocation ke liye kaafi bade nahi hote.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Isse bachne ke liye Redis system ke default \u003ccode\u003emalloc\u003c/code\u003e ko\n        override karke apna custom wrapper\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ezmalloc\u003c/span\u003e chalata hai — yeh background mein\n        Facebook ke \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eje_malloc\u003c/span\u003e ya Google ke\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003etc_malloc\u003c/span\u003e jaise highly-efficient allocators ko\n        invoke karta hai, jo physical memory blocks ko track karke\n        \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003econstant background defragmentation\u003c/span\u003e\n        chalate rehte hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e2️⃣ Intset Optimization (Sorted Integers)\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Jab aap ek normal \u003ccode\u003eSET\u003c/code\u003e banate ho, Redis internally ek\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eHash Table\u003c/span\u003e use karta hai. Lekin agar Set\n        mein \u003cb\u003esirf integers\u003c/b\u003e hain, Redis automatically encoding badal kar\n        use \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eintset\u003c/span\u003e mein upgrade kar deta hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          \u003ccode\u003eintset\u003c/code\u003e memory mein integers ka ek\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eraw sorted array\u003c/span\u003e hota hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Sorted hone ki wajah se search\n          \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003eBinary Search\u003c/span\u003e se hoti hai — complexity\n          sirf \u003cb\u003eO(log N)\u003c/b\u003e.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Agar 16-bit array mein koi bada value (jaise 64-bit integer) insert\n          karo, Redis pura array reallocate + shift karke\n          \u003cb\u003e64-bit array\u003c/b\u003e mein upgrade kar deta hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e \u003ccode\u003eSADD myset 1 2 3\u003c/code\u003e →\n        intset encoding use hoga (chhota, fast). Agar aap\n        \u003ccode\u003eSADD myset "hello"\u003c/code\u003e karo (non-integer), toh Redis pura Set\n        normal Hash Table encoding mein convert kar dega.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Yeh optimization ek limit tak hi rehti hai (config:\n        \u003ccode\u003eset-max-intset-entries\u003c/code\u003e). Uske baad, insertion slow hone se\n        bachne ke liye Set normal Hash Table mein upgrade ho jaata hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e⭐ Interview Point\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eQ:\u003c/b\u003e Redis apne aap encoding kyun badalta rehta hai (intset →\n          hashtable, ya integer-string → raw string)?\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eA:\u003c/b\u003e Kyunki chhoti/simple data ke liye compact encoding (kam\n          memory, fast ops) kaam kar jaata hai, lekin badi/complex data ke liye\n          woh encoding slow ho jaati hai. Redis dynamically best trade-off\n          choose karta hai — \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ememory efficiency vs speed\u003c/span\u003e.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 7 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 8 (was Topic 9) --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-teal"\u003e\n        Topic 8\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\n          \u003eGeniuses of Approximation — LRU, LFU, HyperLogLog, GeoHash\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Heavy databases exact statistics ke liye heavy pointers aur\n        doubly-linked lists use karti hain — jo Redis bilkul nahi karta. Redis\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\n          \u003emathematically sound approximation algorithms\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n        use karta hai jo \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ethoda sa accuracy\u003c/span\u003e chhod kar\n        \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003ebohot saara memory aur speed\u003c/span\u003e bacha lete\n        hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e1️⃣ Approximated LRU Eviction (with code)\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Sahi (exact) LRU ke liye ek doubly-linked list maintain karni padti hai\n        — har access par node ko head par push karna padta hai. Iska pointer\n        management aur RAM consumption bohot zyada hota hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Redis iske badle \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eApproximated LRU\u003c/span\u003e chalata\n        hai — poora algorithm neeche pseudo-code mein dekhiye:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\n          \u003ePseudo-code (JavaScript) — Approximated LRU Eviction\u003c/span\n        \u003e// robj ke andar 24-bit "lru" field already stored hai // (Topic 6 ka\n        LRU/LFU field, jab bhi key access hoti hai update hota hai) function\n        evictIfNeeded() { if (memoryUsed() \u003c= maxMemory) { return; // abhi\n        eviction ki zaroorat nahi } const pool = loadEvictionPool(); // size-16\n        sorted pool (persist across calls) const sample = randomSampleKeys(5);\n        // 🎲 saari keys mein se sirf 5 random keys uthao for (const key of\n        sample) { const idleTime = currentLRUClock() - key.lru;\n        pool.insertSorted(key, idleTime); // pool ko idle-time ke hisaab se\n        sorted rakho if (pool.size() \u003e 16) { pool.removeLowestIdle(); // pool ka\n        size 16 tak hi limit } } const victim = pool.highestIdleTime(); // jiska\n        idle time sabse zyada, wahi target deleteKey(victim); }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003col\n        style="\n          font-size: 19px;\n          line-height: 1.65;\n          color: var(--pencil);\n          padding-left: 26px;\n        "\n      \u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Memory full hone par, Redis \u003cb\u003erandomly 5 keys\u003c/b\u003e ko sample karta hai\n          — \u003cspan class="hl"\u003epoori keyspace scan nahi karta\u003c/span\u003e, yehi speed\n          ka raaz hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Unhe ek sorted \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003e"eviction pool"\u003c/span\u003e (size 16)\n          mein rakhta hai — yeh pool calls ke beech persist karta hai, isliye\n          samples accumulate hote rehte hain.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Jis key ka \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eidle time sabse zyada\u003c/span\u003e hota hai\n          (pool ke top se), use delete kar deta hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ol\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-teal"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e📚 Library Inspector Analogy\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Socho ek \u003cb\u003elibrary hai jisme lakhon books hain\u003c/b\u003e. Ek inspector ko\n          pata karna hai konsi book sabse zyada der se koi nahi chhoo raha (usse\n          hatana hai, jagah banani hai). Har ek book ka exact "last-touched"\n          record rakhna (real/exact LRU — doubly linked list, jaha har access\n          par book ko queue ke sabse aage laana padta hai) bohot mehenga hai —\n          millions of books ke liye pointer maintain karna, RAM aur CPU dono\n          khaata hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eInspector ka jugaad (Approximated LRU):\u003c/span\u003e\n          Wo har round mein sirf \u003cb\u003e5 random books\u003c/b\u003e uthata hai, unka\n          idle-time check karta hai, aur ek\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eshortlist ("eviction pool", size 16)\u003c/span\u003e mein\n          rakhta hai jismein sabse zyada idle books already accumulate ho chuki\n          hain purane rounds se.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eRound 1 — Random Sample (5 keys)\u003c/th\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eIdle Time\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003etoken:3\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e300 sec\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003esession:88\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e120 sec\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003euser:205\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e45 sec\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecache:9\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e5 sec\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e\u003ccode\u003euser:100\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e2 sec\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003c/table\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Ye 5 keys ab existing 16-size pool mein merge hoti hain (jahan pehle\n          se dusre rounds ke samples baithe hain), pool\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eidle-time ke hisaab se sorted rehta hai\u003c/span\u003e. Jo\n          top pe hai (yahan \u003ccode\u003etoken:3\u003c/code\u003e, 300 sec idle) —\n          \u003cb\u003ewahi delete hoti hai\u003c/b\u003e is eviction cycle mein.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eKyun kaam karta hai:\u003c/span\u003e Poori keyspace scan\n          nahi ki — sirf 5 random keys check ki. Aur kyunki pool\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003epersist karta hai calls ke beech\u003c/span\u003e (naye samples\n          purane accumulated samples mein merge hote rehte hain), thodi der mein\n          pool mein genuinely sabse purani/idle keys hi accumulate ho jaati hain\n          — result\n          \u003cspan class="hl-green"\n            \u003ealmost as good as true LRU, fraction ki cost par\u003c/span\n          \u003e. Redis config \u003ccode\u003emaxmemory-samples\u003c/code\u003e se ye sample-size (5)\n          tune bhi kar sakte ho — jitna zyada sample, utna accurate lekin utna\n          hi slow.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-teal"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e⏱️ The 24-Bit Clock Hack\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          24-bit space mein exact absolute timestamp store karna namumkin hai\n          (utni jagah hi nahi). Isliye Redis\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eepoch time ke last 24 bits ko mask\u003c/span\u003e karke apna\n          chhota "lru clock" banata hai, aur wahi robj ke andar store karta hai\n          (yaad karo — Topic 6 mein LRU/LFU field 24 bits ki thi!).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e\n        2️⃣ Morris Counter — LFU (Least Frequently Used) Eviction (with code)\n      \u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        LFU ke liye agar har key ki access frequency exactly track karein, toh\n        ek pura 4-byte integer counter chahiye — millions keys par yeh bohot\n        memory le lega. Redis isko sirf\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003e8 bits\u003c/span\u003e mein squeeze karne ke liye\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eMorris Counter\u003c/span\u003e (logarithmic/probabilistic\n        counter) use karta hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="code-title"\n          \u003ePseudo-code (JavaScript) — Morris Counter (Probabilistic\n          Increment)\u003c/span\n        \u003e// counter sirf 8 bits (0-255) ka hota hai, per-key robj mein stored\n        function logIncrement(counter) { if (counter === 255) { return counter;\n        // max cap, ab aur nahi badhega } // jitna bada counter, utna kam chance\n        increment hone ka const probability = 1.0 / (counter * LFU_FACTOR + 1);\n        if (Math.random() \u003c probability) { counter++; // sirf probability ke\n        hisaab se hi +1 hota hai } return counter; } // Har key access par yeh\n        chalta hai: function onKeyAccess(key) { key.lfu = logIncrement(key.lfu);\n        } // Background: har ~1 minute mein decay bhi chalta hai function\n        decayCounter(counter, minutesIdle) { const decaySteps =\n        Math.floor(minutesIdle / LFU_DECAY_TIME); if (counter \u003e decaySteps) {\n        return counter - decaySteps; // purani/inactive keys ka score ghatao }\n        return 0; }\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-teal"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e✍️ "Reluctant Secretary" Analogy\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Socho ek secretary hai jiska kaam hai —\n          \u003cb\u003ehar baar jab koi office mein aaye\u003c/b\u003e, ek register mein mark\n          lagana, taaki total visitors ka count pata rahe. Lekin agar office\n          mein \u003cb\u003elakhon log roz aate hon\u003c/b\u003e, har ek ke liye mark lagana bohot\n          ink/paper (memory) khaayega.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Toh secretary ek jugaad nikaalti hai:\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\n            \u003ejaise-jaise register ka number badhta jaata hai, wo utna hi\n            "reluctant" (kam willing) ho jaati hai naya mark lagane ke\n            liye\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          — sikka uchhalti hai apne dimaag mein, aur sirf tabhi mark lagati hai\n          jab sikka uske favour mein aaye. Number chhota ho toh sikka almost\n          hamesha favour mein aata hai (mark lag jaata hai), number bada ho toh\n          sikka bohot kam favour mein aata hai (mark rarely lagta hai).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\n            \u003eConcrete probability walkthrough (LFU_FACTOR = 10):\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eCounter Value\u003c/th\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eIncrement Probability = 1 / (counter × 10 + 1)\u003c/th\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eMatlab\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e0\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e1 / 1 = 100%\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003ePehli access par hamesha 0 → 1 hota hai\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e1\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e1 / 11 ≈ 9%\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003eAb 11 mein se sirf 1 baar hi badhega\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e5\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e1 / 51 ≈ 2%\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e50 mein se sirf 1 baar badhega\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e50\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e1 / 501 ≈ 0.2%\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e500 mein se sirf 1 baar badhega\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n          \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e200\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e1 / 2001 ≈ 0.05%\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e2000 mein se sirf 1 baar badhega — bohot rare\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003c/table\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003e20 real accesses simulate karke dekho:\u003c/span\u003e\n          Ek naya key access hote hi counter \u003ccode\u003e0 → 1\u003c/code\u003e ho jaata hai\n          (100% chance). Access #2 se #10 tak counter shayad\n          \u003ccode\u003e1 → 2 → 3\u003c/code\u003e tak hi pahunche (kyunki har step ki probability\n          ghatti jaa rahi hai). Access #20 tak counter mushkil se\n          \u003ccode\u003e4 ya 5\u003c/code\u003e tak pahunchega — jabki real access count 20 hai!\n          \u003cspan class="hl-green"\n            \u003eYehi to point hai — counter access-count ke saath linear nahi,\n            logarithmic (bohot dheere) badhta hai\u003c/span\n          \u003e, isliye chhota sa \u003cb\u003e8-bit (0-255) counter\u003c/b\u003e hi kaafi hota hai —\n          255 tak pahunchne ke liye lakhon-lakhon real accesses chahiye honge.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eKaise kaam karta hai →\u003c/span\u003e Chhoti frequencies\n        par counter \u003cb\u003ehar access par\u003c/b\u003e badhta hai (jaldi jaldi increment,\n        kyunki \u003ccode\u003eprobability\u003c/code\u003e bohot high hoti hai counter chhota hone\n        par). Lekin jaise-jaise frequency badhti jaati hai,\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ecounter badhne ka probability kam hota jaata hai\u003c/span\u003e\n        — yaani 100 se 101 hona easy, lekin 900,000 se 900,001 hona rare event\n        ban jaata hai.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Isi trick se sirf \u003cb\u003e8 bits\u003c/b\u003e ke counter se Redis\n        \u003cb\u003e1 million tak\u003c/b\u003e ka count approximate kar leta hai! Purani/inactive\n        keys ko sahi represent karne ke liye, upar wale code mein dikhaya\n        \u003ccode\u003edecayCounter\u003c/code\u003e function\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ehar 1 minute mein automatically counter kam\u003c/span\u003e\n        karta rehta hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e3️⃣ HyperLogLog — Unique Counts (Cardinality)\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Agar website ke unique visitors count karne hain, toh normal Set mein\n        saari IDs store karna bohot costly hoga.\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eHyperLogLog\u003c/span\u003e is cardinality estimation ko\n        sirf \u003cb\u003eO(log M)\u003c/b\u003e space mein approximate kar deta hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cul\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003eHar incoming element ko \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ehash\u003c/span\u003e karta hai.\u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Binary string mein rightmost set bit ki\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003emaximum trailing-zeros position (B)\u003c/span\u003e ko\n          track karta hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Statistical formula ke hisaab se: agar max trailing zeros\n          \u003cb\u003eB\u003c/b\u003e hain, toh unique items ki sankhya lagbhag \u003cb\u003e2^B\u003c/b\u003e hogi.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ul\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e Agar hashed values mein aapko\n        max trailing zeros \u003cb\u003eB = 20\u003c/b\u003e mile, toh estimate hoga roughly\n        \u003cb\u003e2^20 ≈ 10 lakh (1 million)\u003c/b\u003e unique elements — sirf kuch KB memory\n        mein!\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-teal"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\u003e🪙 "Longest Heads Streak" Coin-Flip Analogy\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Ye samajhna easy ho jaata hai coin-flip se: agar tum kisi se bologe\n          "coin uchhalo jab tak Tail na aaye, phir bata do kitni baar Head aaya\n          lagatar" — aur wo bole \u003cb\u003e"5 Heads lagatar aaye"\u003c/b\u003e — toh tum bina\n          unhe dekhe hi guess kar sakte ho ki\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\n            \u003eunhone kaafi baar (shayad ~32 baar) coin uchhala hoga\u003c/span\n          \u003e, kyunki 5 Heads lagatar aana bohot rare event hai (2^5 = 32 mein se\n          1 chance) — jitni lambi streak, utni zyada attempts hui hongi.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          HyperLogLog isi trick ko reverse mein use karta hai: har incoming\n          element ko \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ehash\u003c/span\u003e karke ek random-dikhne wala\n          binary string milta hai (jaise coin flips ki sequence). Us binary\n          string mein \u003cb\u003etrailing zeros ki ginti\u003c/b\u003e = "Head streak length"\n          jaisi hai. Jitne zyada unique elements aayenge, utna zyada chance hai\n          ki koi ek element bohot lambi trailing-zero streak de — aur us\n          \u003cb\u003emaximum streak (B)\u003c/b\u003e se hum reverse-calculate kar lete hain ki\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003eroughly kitne unique elements the\u003c/span\u003e\n          (≈ 2^B).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\n            \u003eRegisters — accuracy improve karne ka trick:\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          Sirf ek hi "max streak" track karna bohot noisy (unreliable) hota hai\n          — ek lucky element se hi estimate galat ho sakta hai. Isliye real\n          HyperLogLog hash ko do parts mein todta hai: pehle kuch bits se decide\n          karta hai \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ekaunse "register" (bucket)\u003c/span\u003e mein ye\n          element jaayega (typically \u003cb\u003e16,384 registers\u003c/b\u003e), aur baaki bits se\n          us register ke andar trailing-zero streak update karta hai. Phir sab\n          16,384 registers ka \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003eharmonic mean\u003c/span\u003e leke\n          final estimate nikaalta hai — isse randomness ka noise average ho\n          jaata hai, aur error rate sirf \u003cb\u003e~0.81%\u003c/b\u003e tak simat jaata hai —\n          jabki poora structure sirf \u003cb\u003e~12 KB fixed memory\u003c/b\u003e leta hai, chahe\n          cardinality 10 ho ya 10 crore!\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e4️⃣ GeoHash — Geospatial Queries\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        2D coordinates (latitude/longitude) par proximity distance calculate\n        karna mathematically bohot heavy hota hai. Redis iske liye world map ko\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003erecursive binary grids\u003c/span\u003e mein divide karta hai aur\n        lat/long ko \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003einterleave (mix)\u003c/span\u003e karke ek\n        single \u003cb\u003e64-bit integer\u003c/b\u003e bana deta hai — isi ko GeoHash kehte hain.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="callout c-teal"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="tag"\n          \u003e🗺️ "20 Questions" Analogy — Map Ko Aadha-Aadha Karo\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Socho tumhe kisi ki location batani hai bina exact coordinates bole —\n          sirf "Haan/Na" jawaab dekar. Tum poochte jaate ho: "Kya wo world map\n          ke \u003cb\u003eeast half\u003c/b\u003e mein hai?" — jawaab "Haan" mila toh bit\n          \u003ccode\u003e1\u003c/code\u003e likho, warna \u003ccode\u003e0\u003c/code\u003e. Fir us half ko wapas\n          \u003cb\u003enorth/south\u003c/b\u003e mein todte ho, phir agla bit. Ye process baar-baar\n          repeat karte ho —\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ehar baar box chhota hota jaata hai\u003c/span\u003e, aur jitne\n          zyada bits pooche, utni precise location.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          GeoHash bilkul isi tarah kaam karta hai, bas questions\n          \u003cb\u003ealternate hote hain longitude aur latitude ke beech\u003c/b\u003e — pehla bit\n          longitude ke liye (east/west), dusra bit latitude ke liye\n          (north/south), phir wapas longitude, aise hi chalta rehta hai. In sab\n          bits ko ek saath jod (interleave) ke ek single number ban jaata hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\n            \u003eChhota toy example (4×4 grid, sirf samajhne ke liye):\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          Maan lo poora map ek \u003ccode\u003e4×4\u003c/code\u003e grid hai — longitude aur\n          latitude dono ki range ko sirf \u003cb\u003e2 bits\u003c/b\u003e se represent kar rahe\n          hain (0 se 3 tak). Kisi point ka:\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cul\u003e\n          \u003cli\u003e\n            Longitude bits = \u003ccode\u003e10\u003c/code\u003e (binary 2 — grid ke right-half\n            mein)\n          \u003c/li\u003e\n          \u003cli\u003e\n            Latitude bits = \u003ccode\u003e01\u003c/code\u003e (binary 1 — grid ke lower-half mein)\n          \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003c/ul\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Ab in dono ko \u003cspan class="hl"\u003ealternate karke interleave\u003c/span\u003e\n          karte hain (Long-bit, Lat-bit, Long-bit, Lat-bit...):\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="code-box"\u003e\n          Longitude bits: 1 0 Latitude bits: 0 1 Interleaved : 1 0 0 1 → GeoHash\n          = "1001" (binary) = 9\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          Real Redis mein ye same process hota hai, bas \u003cb\u003e52-60 bits\u003c/b\u003e tak\n          (har bit ek aur "half" narrow karta hai), jisse precision centimeters\n          tak ja sakti hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\n            \u003ePrefix matching se proximity kyun kaam karta hai:\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          Do points jo geographically paas hain, unke GeoHash mein\n          \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003eshuru ke kaafi bits same honge\u003c/span\u003e\n          (kyunki dono baar-baar same "half" mein aaye — same east-half, same\n          north-half, etc.). Jaise-jaise bits aage badhte hain, tabhi farak aana\n          shuru hota hai (jab dono points chhote grid boxes mein alag pad jaate\n          hain). Isliye\n          \u003cb\n            \u003ecommon prefix ki length hi "kitna paas hain" ka signal ban jaati\n            hai\u003c/b\n          \u003e\n          — koi trigonometry ya distance formula chalane ki zaroorat nahi, sirf\n          string prefix compare karo!\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cspan class="arrow-note"\u003e⚠️ Ek chhoti si limitation:\u003c/span\u003e Kabhi\n          kabhi do points geographically bilkul paas hote hain lekin ek grid\n          \u003cb\u003eboundary line ke exact do taraf\u003c/b\u003e aa jaate hain (jaise ek east\n          half ke andar hai, dusra bilkul border cross karke west half mein) —\n          tab unke prefixes match nahi karenge chahe wo meters door hon. Isliye\n          real GeoHash-based search (jaise Redis \u003ccode\u003eGEOSEARCH\u003c/code\u003e) sirf ek\n          prefix nahi,\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\n            \u003eaas-paas ke neighbouring cells bhi check karta hai\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          is edge-case ko handle karne ke liye.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eFayda →\u003c/span\u003e Isse complex proximity search ek\n        simple \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eprefix match\u003c/span\u003e mein badal jaati hai, aur\n        zoom in/out karna sirf right side ke bits manipulate karne jaisa aasan\n        ho jaata hai.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e⭐ Interview Point\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eQ:\u003c/b\u003e LRU aur LFU mein core difference kya hai, aur Redis dono ko\n          approximate kyun karta hai?\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003eA:\u003c/b\u003e LRU "sabse purana access" track karta hai (recency), LFU\n          "sabse zyada access" track karta hai (frequency). Dono ko exact rakhna\n          (doubly-linked list ya bade counters) bohot memory/CPU costly hai —\n          isliye Redis random sampling (LRU) aur probabilistic counting\n          (LFU/Morris Counter) se\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\u003e"kaafi accurate, bohot sasta"\u003c/span\u003e trade-off leta\n          hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 8 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 9 (was Topic 10) --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003ch2 class="topic c-teal"\u003e\n        Topic 9\n        \u003cspan class="topic-tag"\n          \u003eServer Ko Zinda Rakhna — Pipelining, Persistence, Shutdown\u003c/span\n        \u003e\n      \u003c/h2\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e1️⃣ Command Pipelining — Network Boost\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Normal flow mein client ek command bhejta hai aur server ke respond hone\n        ka wait karta hai — yeh\n        \u003cspan class="hl"\u003eRTT (Round-Trip-Time) delay\u003c/span\u003e add karta hai har\n        command par.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003ePipelining\u003c/span\u003e mein client multiple commands\n        ko \u003cb\u003eek hi network packet\u003c/b\u003e mein append karke bhej deta hai. Redis\n        unhe sequentially execute karta hai, saari responses ko in-memory buffer\n        mein hold karta hai, aur end mein\n        \u003cspan class="hl-green"\u003epoori response list ek saath\u003c/span\u003e network par\n        flush kar deta hai.\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="example-box"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="ex-label"\u003eExample →\u003c/span\u003e 1000 \u003ccode\u003eSET\u003c/code\u003e commands\n        normally 1000 round-trips lete — pipelining se sab ek packet mein bhej\n        do, aur ek hi round-trip mein saari responses wapas aa jaati hain.\n        Network context-switches aur system calls drastically kam ho jaate hain\n        → throughput badh jaati hai.\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e2️⃣ Persistence — RDB vs AOF\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Memory \u003cspan class="hl"\u003evolatile\u003c/span\u003e hoti hai (power jaane par data\n        gayab) — isliye durability ke liye Redis ke paas do tareeke hain:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="sticky-row"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky blue"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-teal"\u003eRDB (Snapshot)\u003c/b\u003e\n          Poore database ka point-in-time highly-compressed snapshot. Main\n          thread block na ho, isliye Redis ek naya process \u003cb\u003efork\u003c/b\u003e karta hai\n          jo background mein disk par dump karta hai — main thread clients serve\n          karta rehta hai.\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sticky green"\u003e\n          \u003cb class="c-teal"\u003eAOF (Append-Only File)\u003c/b\u003e\n          Raw write-log — har modify command RESP format mein append hota hai.\n          File badi ho sakti hai isliye background mein\n          \u003ccode\u003eBGREWRITEAOF\u003c/code\u003e chalta hai jo minimal commands likhta hai\n          (10 changes hue toh sirf final state ka 1 SET likhega).\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003ctable class="cmp-table"\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eAspect\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eRDB\u003c/th\u003e\n          \u003cth\u003eAOF\u003c/th\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eKya hai\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eCompressed snapshot (point-in-time)\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eHar command ka log (append-only)\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eKaise banta hai\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eFork karke background process\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eHar write par log mein append\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eFile size\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eChhoti (compressed)\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eBadi ho sakti hai, isliye rewrite hoti hai\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eDurability\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eKam (last snapshot ke baad ka data lose ho sakta hai)\u003c/td\u003e\n          \u003ctd\u003eZyada (near real-time log)\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n      \u003c/table\u003e\n\n      \u003ch3 class="sub-heading"\u003e3️⃣ Graceful Shutdown — Safe Exit\u003c/h3\u003e\n      \u003cp\u003e\n        Jab aap server ko \u003ccode\u003eCtrl+C\u003c/code\u003e (\u003cspan class="hl"\n          \u003eSIGINT/SIGTERM\u003c/span\n        \u003e) bhejte ho, data aur socket corruption se bachne ke liye Redis\n        \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003egraceful termination\u003c/span\u003e execute karta hai:\n      \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003col\n        style="\n          font-size: 19px;\n          line-height: 1.65;\n          color: var(--pencil);\n          padding-left: 26px;\n        "\n      \u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Single-threaded hone ki wajah se, Redis pehle\n          \u003cspan class="hl"\n            \u003ecurrently chal rahi client command ke complete hone ka wait\u003c/span\n          \u003e\n          karta hai — taaki transactional state safe rahe.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          Node.js implementation mein iske liye ek\n          \u003cspan class="bold-ink"\u003e"shutting-down" flag\u003c/span\u003e aur\n          pending-operations counter use hote hain (JS single-threaded hai,\n          isliye Go jaisi channels/CAS ki zaroorat nahi padti — bas ek shared\n          flag engine status track kar leta hai) taaki shutdown ke beech koi\n          naya connection busy state mein na aa jaaye.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n        \u003cli\u003e\n          In-memory buffers disk par flush hote hain, final backup snapshot\n          banta hai, open connections close hoti hain, aur process safely exit\n          ho jaati hai.\n        \u003c/li\u003e\n      \u003c/ol\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="interview-star"\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="star-title"\u003e✅ Sab Phases Complete!\u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003ePhase 1:\u003c/b\u003e TCP blocking model + RESP wire protocol (5 types +\n          binary safety + delta parsing).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003ePhase 2:\u003c/b\u003e Traditional DB locking nightmare vs Redis event-loop +\n          Packet lifecycle (Hardware → Interrupt → Kernel Buffer → User Space) +\n          epoll_create1/ctl/wait + FD abstraction + kqueue/IOCP cross-platform.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n        \u003cp\u003e\n          \u003cb\u003ePhase 3:\u003c/b\u003e robj 12-byte structure + zmalloc/intset + Approximated\n          LRU/Morris LFU/HyperLogLog/GeoHash + Pipelining + RDB vs AOF +\n          Graceful shutdown.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e— page 9 —\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- PAGE 10: GLOSSARY --\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class="page"\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="title-block"\u003e\n        \u003ch1 class="c-red" style="font-size: 36px"\u003eBhaari Words Glossary 📖\u003c/h1\u003e\n        \u003cdiv class="sub"\u003e\n          Jo bhi complex/technical terms upar aaye — unka seedha-saada matlab\n          yahan\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eFile Descriptor (FD)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Unix/Linux mein har open connection (socket, file) ko represent karne\n          wala ek chhota integer number — OS isi number se track karta hai ki\n          kaunsi connection kis baare mein hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eBlocking Call\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Aisi function call jo tab tak return nahi karti jab tak uska kaam\n          poora na ho jaaye — us dauran thread kahin aur kaam nahi kar sakta\n          (jaise \u003ccode\u003eread()\u003c/code\u003e jab tak data na aaye).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eSerialize / Serialization\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Data ko ek structured, transferable format (jaise RESP ya JSON) mein\n          convert karna, taaki use network par bheja ja sake aur dusri taraf\n          wapas parse kiya ja sake.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eBinary Safe\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Aisa format jo kisi bhi tarah ke bytes (chahe unme null characters ya\n          special characters ho) ko bina galat interpret kiye handle kar sake —\n          kyunki length pehle se pata hoti hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eConcurrency\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Ek hi waqt mein multiple kaamon ko "handle" karne ki capability —\n          zaroori nahi ki woh sach mein simultaneously ho rahe hon (Redis mein\n          yeh \u003ci\u003eapparent\u003c/i\u003e/lagta hua parallelism hai, actual mein single\n          thread hi hai).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eParallelism\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Sach mein ek saath, alag-alag CPU cores par, do kaam chalna (jaise\n          traditional multi-threaded DB mein hota hai).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eMutex / Semaphore (Lock)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Ek "taala" jo ek waqt mein sirf ek thread ko shared data touch karne\n          deta hai — baaki sab wait karte hain, taaki data corrupt na ho.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eRace Condition\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Jab do threads ek hi shared data ko ek sath modify karne ki koshish\n          karein, aur result unke execution ke "timing/order" par depend kar\n          jaaye — jisse galat/unpredictable answer aa sakta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eContext-Switching\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          CPU ka ek thread ka kaam rok kar dusre thread ka register-state load\n          karna — is switching mein bhi thoda time/energy lagta hai, jo "useful\n          kaam" nahi hai, sirf overhead hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eAsynchronous I/O\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Aisa tareeka jisme thread kisi I/O operation (read/write) ke complete\n          hone ka intezaar "block" hoke nahi karta — balki kernel se poochta\n          rehta hai "ready hua kya?" aur tab tak aur kaam kar sakta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eI/O Multiplexing\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Ek hi thread se multiple I/O sources (sockets) ko ek saath "monitor"\n          karna — kernel batata hai kaunsa ready hai, taaki ek thread sabko\n          cover kar sake.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eKernel Space vs User Space\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Kernel space = OS ka private, protected memory area (aapka code direct\n          access nahi kar sakta). User space = aapka normal program jahan chalta\n          hai. Security ke liye dono alag rakhe jaate hain, isliye data copy\n          karke ek se dusre mein bhejna padta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eHardware Interrupt\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Jab network card ya koi hardware CPU ko "signal" bhejta hai ki "mujhe\n          turant attention chahiye" — CPU apna chal raha kaam rok kar pehle isko\n          handle karta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eBit Field\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          C programming ka ek trick jisme ek variable ke andar sirf kuch\n          specific "bits" ko ek chhote field ke liye reserve kiya jaata hai\n          (jaise 4 bits sirf "type" batane ke liye) — isse memory bohot bachti\n          hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eRefcount (Reference Count)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Kitni jagah se ek hi object ko "use"/point kiya ja raha hai, uska\n          counter. Jab yeh 0 ho jaaye, matlab koi use nahi kar raha — memory\n          free kar do (garbage collection ka simplest form).\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eMemory Fragmentation\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Baar-baar chhote data allocate/delete karne se memory mein bikhri hui\n          chhoti khaali jagah ban jaati hai — jo individually kisi bade naye\n          data ke kaam nahi aati, isliye RAM "waste" lagti hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eAllocator (malloc/zmalloc/je_malloc/tc_malloc)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Woh program/library jo decide karta hai ki naya data memory mein\n          \u003ci\u003ekahaan\u003c/i\u003e aur \u003ci\u003ekaise\u003c/i\u003e store hoga — efficient allocators\n          fragmentation kam karte hain aur speed badhate hain.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eBinary Search — O(log N)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Sorted data mein baar-baar beech se "aadha-aadha" data discard karte\n          hue search karna — bohot fast, kyunki har step data ka half hissa hi\n          check karna padta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eApproximation Algorithm\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Aisa algorithm jo 100% exact answer nahi deta, par bohot kam\n          memory/time mein "kaafi close" answer de deta hai — Redis inhe\n          jaan-bujh kar use karta hai kyunki exact answer ka kharcha bohot zyada\n          hota.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eEviction\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Memory full hone par purani/kam-zaroori keys ko database se\n          automatically delete karna, taaki naye data ke liye jagah bane.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eProbabilistic Counter (jaise Morris Counter)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Aisa counter jo har baar guaranteed +1 nahi karta — balki ek\n          "probability/chance" ke hisaab se badhta hai, taaki chhote se space\n          mein bade numbers approximate ho sakein.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eDecay\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Time ke saath kisi value (jaise LFU counter) ka dheere-dheere kam hote\n          jaana — taaki purani activity ka असर hamesha ke liye na rahe, aur\n          recent activity zyada priority paaye.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eCardinality\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Kisi set mein "unique" (bina repeat ke) items ki total sankhya — jaise\n          website ke unique visitors ki ginti.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eHashing\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Kisi bhi input (chhota ya bada) ko ek fixed-size random-dikhne wale\n          number/string mein convert karna — same input hamesha same hash dega.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eInterleave\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Do alag values (jaise latitude aur longitude) ke bits ko baari-baari\n          "mix" karke ek single combined number banana — GeoHash isi trick se\n          banta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eRTT (Round-Trip Time)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Client se request bhejne se lekar server ka response wapas aane tak jo\n          total time lagta hai — network delay ka core measure.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eFork (Process Fork)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Ek chal rahe process ki hoobahoo copy banana (naya independent\n          process) — RDB snapshot ke liye Redis isi trick se background mein\n          disk-write karta hai bina main thread ko rok kar.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eSIGINT / SIGTERM\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          OS ke special "signals" jo kisi running program ko bolte hain "ab band\n          ho jao" — SIGINT wahi hai jo Ctrl+C dabane par milta hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eCompare-And-Swap (CAS)\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Ek atomic (single, uninterruptible) operation jo kehti hai: "agar\n          current value X hai toh use Y se badal do, warna kuch mat karo" — bina\n          lock ke safe updates ke liye use hota hai.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n      \u003cdiv class="glossary-item"\u003e\n        \u003cspan class="term"\u003eC10K Problem\u003c/span\u003e\n        \u003cp class="meaning"\u003e\n          Purani server architectures (thread-per-connection) ke liye 10,000\n          concurrent connections handle karna practically mushkil tha —\n          epoll/kqueue jaisi techniques isi problem ko solve karne ke liye bani.\n        \u003c/p\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n\n      \u003cdiv class="page-footer"\u003e\n        — page 10 — Glossary Complete ✅ — Ab revision ke liye kahin aur jaane\n        ki zaroorat nahi! —\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003c/div\u003e\n  ';
